  {
   "id": "crush-variables-md-1",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "# Variables and Scoping\n\nThis chapter covers declaration, assignment, and the scoping rules the compiler\nenforces today.\n\n## Declaration\n\nEvery variable is introduced with `let` and **must be initialised**:\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-variables-1",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "let x = 42\nlet name = \"Alice\"\nlet active = true\nprint(x)\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "runnable"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/variables.md:10",
      "browser": "run",
      "expect_output": "42\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-variables-md-3",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "A bare `let x` (no value) is a parse error. So is `let mut x = 0` — `mut` is a\nreserved word, but it is not accepted in a declaration, and there is no `const`.\nEvery `let` variable is simply reassignable.\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-variables-2",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "fn main() {\n    let x\n}\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "expected-failure"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/variables.md:27",
      "browser": "fail",
      "why": "this example shows a compile error"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-variables-3",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "fn main() {\n    let mut counter = 0\n}\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "expected-failure"
    ],
    "extra": {
